// Conformance row V86 — reading `self` out of a BORROWED receiver and handing
// it to a new owner is a TRANSFER, and takes the transfer checkpoint
//
// SL-218, closed by design 269 (SL-211). `_is_aliasing_expr` was a node-type
// test over four classes plus two hand-added transparencies, and `SelfExpr` was
// on none of the lists — so a receiver read was judged a FRESH TEMPORARY at
// every tier arm: NoCopy and ExplicitCopy skipped their refusal and the Copy
// tier skipped its `needs_copy` stamp. Same mechanism as DF-216a (the
// synthesized call constructions that skipped the checkpoint) and DF-299a
// (`CastExpr`), which is why it was filed as a class and fixed as one: the
// classification is now TOTAL over every value-position node
// (`typechecker/producers.py`), gated by `tools/test_producer_taxonomy.py`, and
// `SelfExpr` is one row of it.
//
// A `&self` receiver names storage the CALLER owns — that is what a shared
// borrow IS — so handing it on by value mints a second owner of one value.
// Today every face below compiles and the program prints `drop 1` twice, at
// exit 0; at the ExplicitCopy tier the same shape SIGABRTs on the second free
// of one `Vector` buffer. Codegen hides the Copy tier's half of it: its own
// `_transfer_needs_copy` carries `SelfExpr` in a SEPARATE isinstance list and
// takes the retain there (`Arc.strong_count()` reads 1 -> 2 correctly), so the
// hole is invisible on the tier a second opinion can paper over and fatal on
// the two it cannot. That asymmetry is SL-209's thesis in one program.
//
// SIX FACES, all measured (SL-218 carries the evidence): a return, a by-value
// argument, a `let` binding, a struct field, a tuple element, and a `&var self`
// receiver. Three are below; the argument face is FIRST so the assertion below
// is the ordinary non-return wording. The return face's own refusal reads
// `cannot return NoCopy type ... without `move` in method ...`, which is the
// same arm one branch over.
//
// The way out is not a `move`: a borrowed receiver has nothing to move (the
// value is the caller's), so an author who means to duplicate declares a
// duplicable tier and spells `.copy()`, and one who means to consume declares
// the method `consumes` (design 260). That is DF-290a's shape at the receiver.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: cannot copy value of type `Res` which implements NoCopy

struct Res { w: Int }
extension Res: NoCopy {
    func deinit(&var self) { print("drop {}", self.w) }
}

struct Holder { r: Res }
extension Holder: NoCopy {}

func sink(r: Res) -> Int { r.w }

extension Res {
    // The ARGUMENT face: `self` handed to a by-value parameter.
    func hand_on(&self) -> Int { sink(self) }
    // The BINDING face: `self` read into a local the body then owns.
    func to_local(&self) -> Int { let b = self  b.w }
    // The AGGREGATE face: `self` stored into a struct field.
    func to_field(&self) -> Int { let h = Holder(r: self)  h.r.w }
}

func main() {
    let a = Res(w: 1)
    print("arg {}", a.hand_on())
    let b = Res(w: 2)
    print("local {}", b.to_local())
    let c = Res(w: 3)
    print("field {}", c.to_field())
}
